 if someone else Pengs, and no one has shown interest, keeping both may be overkill.\n\nKey considerations:\n- I lack concealed structure. Any win will likely be low-scoring unless I draw into strong patterns.\n- Safe discards are important. J2 is gone (0 left), so Dragon-related patterns involving Green Dragon are off the table.\n- Opponents have built high-end sequences: previous player has [W5,W6,W7], across has [T7,T8,T9], suggesting Ten-Thousand and Bamboo focus. T8 was frequently discarded \u2014 possibly safe.\n- My best internal pairing is **B8 pair** or **F3 pair**. Holding both gives flexibility.\n\nReason: Among valid actions, **Play T1** stands out as the safest discard. It\u2019s a terminal suited tile, not part of any existing or potential Chow in my hand. No visible opponent is collecting T1 (no chow involving 1-2-3 in Bamboo formed yet), and it doesn't support knitted straights directly in a threatening way. Compared to discarding W-series (which might feed the player across or previous player who\u2019ve shown W activity), T1 is less dangerous. Also, preserving F3 (for possible Seat Wind pair) and B8 (as a potential pair or part of Upper/Lower Four) is wise.\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
