e.\n   - W8-W9: need W7 \u2192 but previous player has [W5,W6,W7] \u2014 they have W7 in meld \u2192 only 4 copies \u2014 they used 3 \u2014 one left. Unshown W7:1 \u2014 so one remains. Possible.\n   - T9: I have one \u2014 but across player has [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 they used T9 \u2014 so one copy used \u2014 3 left. But I have one \u2014 so 2 others somewhere.\n   - But T9 alone \u2014 no sequence unless I get T7-T8.\n\nAlternatively, discard a terminal to reduce risk.\n\nTop candidates for discard:\n- **T9**: Opponent has already melded [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 very likely they will discard T9 again or not need it \u2014 but if they are completing hand, they may need another T9 for pair/kong. Still, T9 is risky to keep \u2014 I can\u2019t use it in any chow without T7/T8.\n- **W1**: Only useful with W2-W3 \u2014 I have W3, so W1+W3 + W2 \u2192 [W1,W2,W3]. Possible.\n- **W3**: Same \u2014 could help form [W1,W2,W3] or [W2,W3,W4]. But I have W1 \u2014 so better to keep W1 and W3 than just one.\n- **B1**: Isolated \u2014 no connection to [B3,B4,B5] chow. Can\u2019t form [B1,B2,B3] without B2. B2 was discarded by me and others \u2014 limited availability.\n- **W8**: Could form [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 I have W9 \u2014 need W7. One W7 unshown \u2014 possible, but risky.\n- **B4**: Cannot discard \u2014 part of meld.\n- **F3**: Pair of seat wind \u2014 worth keeping for 2 points. Don\u2019t discard unless forced.\n\nSo weakest tiles: T9, B1, W1, W8 \u2014 but W1 and W8 have potential.\n\nBest keepers:\n- F3 (pair, Seat Wind)\n- B8 (pair, could grow)\n- W1/W3 (potential [W1,W2,W3])\n- W8/W9 (potential [W7,W8,W9])\n\nBut I can only keep so many.\n\nNeed to discard one.\n\nNow, valid actions: [Play W1; Play B4; Play W3; Play W8; Play B1; Play T9; Play B8; Play F3; Play W9]\n\nCannot Play B4 \u2014 it's part of a meld \u2014 you can't discard a tile from a meld once formed. Similarly, in standard rules, melded tiles are exposed and cannot be discarded. So \"Play B4\" should not be valid \u2014 but it's listed. Perhaps \"Play\" here means discard from hand \u2014 but B4 is in meld, not in hand.\n\nWait: My hand tiles: [W1:1, W3:1, W8:1, W9:1, T9:1, B1:1, B4:1, B8:2, F3:2]\n\nIt lists B4:1 in hand \u2014 but I also have meld [[B3,B4,B5]] \u2014 contradiction.\n\nAh! This is critical.\n\nIf I have a meld [B3,B4,B5], that means I used B4 in a Chow \u2014 so I shouldn't have another B4 in hand \u2014 unless I drew a second B4 later.\n\nBut possible: I formed the chow using B3,B4,B5 from hand, then later drew another B4 \u2014 so now I have an extra B4 in hand.\n\nYes \u2014 allowed. So hand contains leftover tiles, including another B4.\n\nSo I have:\n- Meld: [B3,B4,B5] (exposed)\n- Hand: includes another B4 \u2192 so I have B4 in hand \u2014 can discard it.\n\nBut is that strategic?\n\nHaving an extra B4 \u2014 it's a simple tile \u2014 not terminal or honor. Already used in a chow \u2014 so not useful for another chow unless I try to build [B4,B4,B4] \u2014 but no advantage.\n\nAnd B4 is in the middle \u2014 doesn't help Outside Hand.\n\nMoreover, I have [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 so B4 is already used \u2014 the spare B4 is redundant.\n\nCompare to T9: opponent has [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 so they may be collecting T9 for pair or kong \u2014 dangerous to discard T9 now \u2014 could feed them.\n\nSimilarly, W9: I have one \u2014 could be used in [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 previous player has W7 \u2014 but discarded W8 and W9 \u2014 so maybe not interested.\n\nBut they discarded W9 \u2014 so perhaps safe.\n\nStill, better to keep W9 for potential high chow.\n\nF3: pair of seat wind \u2014 valuable \u2014 do not discard.\n\nB8: pair \u2014 could become pung \u2014 better to keep.\n\nB1: isolated \u2014 no connection \u2014 and B1 is terminal \u2014 but no way to use in chow without B2-B3 \u2014 B3 is in my meld \u2014 but I could form [B1,B2,B3] only if I had B2 \u2014 but I don't \u2014 and B2 has been discarded by me and others \u2014 low availability.\n\nSo B1 is dead tile.\n\nSimilarly, W1: needs W2 \u2014 W2 unshown:3 \u2014 still 3 out there \u2014 possible.\n\nW3: same.\n\nBut between W1 and B1, W1 has more potential.\n\nT9: high risk \u2014 across player has [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 likely waiting for T9 to win \u2014 so discard T9 is dangerous.\n\nTherefore, safest discard is **B1** \u2014 no connections, no value, terminal but useless.\n\nAlternatively, **W1** \u2014 but W1+W3 gives flexibility.\n\nOr **B4** \u2014 spare middle tile \u2014 also low value.\n\nBetween B1 and B4: both are low-value simples.\n\nBut B1 is terminal \u2014 could theoretically be used in [B1,B2,B3] \u2014 but I already have B3,B4,B5 \u2014 and B3 is used \u2014 so I can't reuse B3.\n\nUnless I break the meld \u2014 can't.\n\nSo I cannot use B1 in any chow because B2 is scarce (unshown:2, but already discarded by me and others \u2014 possibly gone).\n\nSimilarly, B4 is already duplicated \u2014 spare B4 is useless.\n\nBut B4 is in a meld \u2014 having extra might allow kong later \u2014 but I would need two more B4 \u2014 only one unshown (total 4: one in meld, one in hand, two elsewhere \u2014 unshown B4:3? List says B4:3 \u2014 yes \u2014 so 3 unshown \u2014 meaning one in meld and hand not counted? No.\n\nUnshown B4:3 \u2014 total B4:4 \u2014 so one must be revealed.\n\nBreakdown:\n- I have one B4 in hand, one in meld \u2192 2 used\n- So 2 left \u2192 unshown should be 2 \u2014 but list says B4:3 \u2192 contradiction.\n\nUnless \"Unshown\" excludes nothing \u2014 total remaining unseen \u2014 so if 2 are in my possession (one in meld, one in hand), and 2 in wall/other hands, unshown = 2 \u2014 but listed as 3.\n\nInconsistency.\n\nPerhaps \"Unshown\" counts only tiles not in any player's hand or discards \u2014 i.e., in the wall.\n\nBut then it should be small numbers.\n\nGiven confusion, better to rely on observed discards and melds.\n\nFrom game history:\n- Discarded B4: none\n- Melds: only my [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 so at least one B4 used\n- I have another B4 in